## The idea

Let d(i, j) be the edit distance between the first i letters of a and the first j letters of b. If the last letters match, nothing needs doing: d(i, j) = d(i−1, j−1). If not, the last step was a **replace** (d(i−1, j−1) + 1), a **delete** (d(i−1, j) + 1) or an **insert** (d(i, j−1) + 1) — take the cheapest. Fill the table row by row; each row only needs the one above.

## Step by step

1. Row 0: turning "" into b[..j] takes j inserts.
2. For each i: cur[0] = i (i deletes).
3. For each j: if the letters match, cur[j] = prev[j−1]; else 1 + min(prev[j−1], prev[j], cur[j−1]).
4. Swap rows. The answer is the last cell.

## Worked example

**Input:** a = "horse", b = "ros"

| Step | What happens |
|---|---|
| h vs r | different → 1 |
| ho vs ro | o = o → copy d(h, r) = 1 |
| hor vs ros | best of replace/insert/delete → 2 |
| horse vs ros | …the corner of the table is 3 |

**Result:** 3 (horse → rorse → rose → ros)

## Cost

O(n · m) time, O(m) memory with two rows.

## The code (C#, compiled and tested against every part of the question)

```csharp
// LeetCode 72 — the fewest single-character inserts, deletes and replaces that turn a into b.
public static class EditDistance {
    public static int MinOps(string a, string b) {
        a ??= ""; b ??= "";
        // prev[j] = distance between the first i-1 characters of a and the first j of b.
        // Only the previous row is needed, so memory is O(|b|), not O(|a| x |b|).
        var prev = new int[b.Length + 1];
        var cur = new int[b.Length + 1];
        for (int j = 0; j <= b.Length; j++) prev[j] = j;            // "" -> b[..j] needs j inserts

        for (int i = 1; i <= a.Length; i++) {
            cur[0] = i;                                              // a[..i] -> "" needs i deletes
            for (int j = 1; j <= b.Length; j++) {
                if (a[i - 1] == b[j - 1]) {
                    cur[j] = prev[j - 1];                            // same letter: nothing to do
                } else {
                    int replace = prev[j - 1], delete = prev[j], insert = cur[j - 1];
                    cur[j] = 1 + Math.Min(replace, Math.Min(delete, insert));
                }
            }
            var t = prev; prev = cur; cur = t;                       // this row becomes the previous one
        }
        return prev[b.Length];
    }
}
```

## Likely follow-up questions

**Q: Different costs for each operation?**

Same recurrence with weights instead of 1.

**Q: Only inserts and deletes?**

Then it is n + m − 2·LCS.

**Q: Complexity?**

O(n·m) time, O(min(n, m)) memory if you put the shorter word in the columns.

**Q: Recover the actual edits?**

Keep the full table and walk back from the corner.

**Q: Shared state?**

None.

**Q: Compare a word against a million dictionary words.**

Do not run the full DP each time: a BK-tree, or a bound — only words within k edits, with a banded DP of width 2k+1.

**Q: Where does this show up?**

Spell-check suggestions and InterviewDB Q8 (word transformation), which is the graph view of single-letter edits.
